1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,168
March 30, 2020.

2
00:00:02,168 --> 00:00:06,218
A white ship, nearly 900 feet long, with red crosses

3
00:00:06,218 --> 00:00:09,411
on its sides, sails into New York Harbor.

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:11,417
Inside it is a hospital.

5
00:00:11,417 --> 00:00:12,455
A thousand beds.

6
00:00:12,455 --> 00:00:14,855
Twelve operating rooms. A CT scanner.

7
00:00:14,855 --> 00:00:17,579
And two plants that make their own oxygen.

8
00:00:18,029 --> 00:00:20,859
Five days earlier, it was still loading supplies at

9
00:00:20,859 --> 00:00:21,913
a pier in Virginia.

10
00:00:21,913 --> 00:00:23,911
Now it had come to a city in crisis.

11
00:00:24,511 --> 00:00:27,450
How do you build a hospital that can sail anywhere

12
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:30,036
in the world, and switch it on in five days?

13
00:00:30,636 --> 00:00:32,637
This is the Navy's floating hospital.

14
00:00:35,237 --> 00:00:36,609
Start with the ship itself.

15
00:00:36,609 --> 00:00:38,693
Because it wasn't built to be a hospital.

16
00:00:39,143 --> 00:00:41,443
Comfort began life as a supertanker.

17
00:00:41,443 --> 00:00:44,509
It's one of two Mercy-class hospital ships, both

18
00:00:44,509 --> 00:00:47,512
converted San Clemente-class tankers, built and

19
00:00:47,512 --> 00:00:50,579
converted by the National Steel and Shipbuilding

20
00:00:50,579 --> 00:00:51,985
Company, in San Diego.

21
00:00:52,435 --> 00:00:56,182
Its sister ship, Mercy, was delivered in 1986.

22
00:00:56,182 --> 00:01:00,417
Comfort was delivered to the Navy's Military Sealift

23
00:01:00,417 --> 00:01:02,698
Command on December 1, 1987.

24
00:01:03,148 --> 00:01:05,959
Mercy's homeport is San Diego, California.

25
00:01:05,959 --> 00:01:08,035
Comfort's is Norfolk, Virginia.

26
00:01:08,035 --> 00:01:11,248
It moved there from the Washington area in 2013.

27
00:01:11,698 --> 00:01:14,346
The numbers are those of a tanker.

28
00:01:14,346 --> 00:01:15,436
894 feet long.

29
00:01:15,436 --> 00:01:16,527
106 feet wide.

30
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:19,330
More than 69,000 tons, fully loaded.

31
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:23,147
One steam turbine, and a top speed of 17.5 knots.

32
00:01:23,597 --> 00:01:26,458
The ship itself isn't run by Navy sailors.

33
00:01:26,458 --> 00:01:29,797
It's operated by about 70 civil service mariners.

34
00:01:29,797 --> 00:01:33,271
The hospital is staffed by up to 1,200 Navy medical

35
00:01:33,271 --> 00:01:35,247
and communications personnel.

36
00:01:35,697 --> 00:01:38,006
Most of the time, the ship waits.

37
00:01:38,006 --> 00:01:41,504
It's kept in what the Navy calls reduced operating

38
00:01:41,504 --> 00:01:45,002
status, with a small crew of civilian mariners and

39
00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:47,031
active-duty personnel aboard.

40
00:01:47,481 --> 00:01:50,584
But when it's called, the Navy says it can be fully

41
00:01:50,584 --> 00:01:52,896
activated and crewed within five days.

42
00:01:53,696 --> 00:01:55,238
So what's actually on board?

43
00:01:55,688 --> 00:01:58,793
According to the Navy, the hospital has twelve fully

44
00:01:58,793 --> 00:02:01,420
equipped operating rooms, and a thousand-bed

45
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:02,495
hospital facility.

46
00:02:02,945 --> 00:02:06,433
It has digital radiology, a medical laboratory, a

47
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:09,637
pharmacy, an optometry lab, and a CT scanner.

48
00:02:10,087 --> 00:02:10,873
A 2017 U.S.

49
00:02:10,873 --> 00:02:14,517
Army video caption describes it as a seagoing level

50
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:18,019
three medical treatment facility, able to quickly

51
00:02:18,019 --> 00:02:21,735
provide basic surgery, care after surgery, and acute

52
00:02:21,735 --> 00:02:25,879
medical care, for humanitarian crises and disaster relief.

53
00:02:26,329 --> 00:02:29,674
An older fact sheet for Comfort describes one of the

54
00:02:29,674 --> 00:02:32,955
largest trauma facilities in the United States, and

55
00:02:32,955 --> 00:02:35,271
a blood supply of up to 5,000 units.

56
00:02:35,721 --> 00:02:38,726
At sea, patients can come aboard through side ports.

57
00:02:38,726 --> 00:02:39,651
Or from the sky.

58
00:02:39,651 --> 00:02:42,657
The flight deck can land large military helicopters.

59
00:02:43,107 --> 00:02:45,204
And the same deck moves supplies.

60
00:02:45,204 --> 00:02:48,127
Here, during a 2025 mission, a Navy helicopter

61
00:02:48,127 --> 00:02:50,797
carries pallets from the ship to the pier.

62
00:02:51,597 --> 00:02:53,893
Hospital ships are older than you might think.

63
00:02:54,343 --> 00:02:57,317
During the Civil War, a ship called Red Rover is

64
00:02:57,317 --> 00:03:00,353
often credited as the Navy's first hospital ship.

65
00:03:00,353 --> 00:03:03,265
It was the first to use women volunteer nurses.

66
00:03:03,715 --> 00:03:06,772
In 1920, the Navy commissioned USS Relief.

67
00:03:06,772 --> 00:03:10,411
According to Navy history, it's the only Navy ship

68
00:03:10,411 --> 00:03:14,123
ever built from the keel up as a floating hospital.

69
00:03:14,123 --> 00:03:15,287
It had 500 beds.

70
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:19,072
And in 1921, it sailed with eleven nurses, the first

71
00:03:19,072 --> 00:03:22,347
Navy women ever assigned to a ship's company.

72
00:03:22,797 --> 00:03:26,014
Today's Comfort is the third ship to carry that name.

73
00:03:26,014 --> 00:03:28,806
The earlier ones served in the two world wars.

74
00:03:29,606 --> 00:03:32,803
And since 1990, this Comfort has been called again

75
00:03:32,803 --> 00:03:33,442
and again.

76
00:03:33,892 --> 00:03:38,105
In the Gulf War, from August 1990 to April 1991, the

77
00:03:38,105 --> 00:03:41,102
ship treated wounded American troops.

78
00:03:41,102 --> 00:03:45,234
Its staff saw more than 8,000 outpatients, admitted

79
00:03:45,234 --> 00:03:49,446
700 inpatients, and performed 337 complex surgeries.

80
00:03:49,896 --> 00:03:53,321
In 1994, it went to work for Haitian migrants.

81
00:03:53,321 --> 00:03:57,117
First, off Kingston, Jamaica, as the first American

82
00:03:57,117 --> 00:04:00,095
floating center for processing migrants.

83
00:04:00,095 --> 00:04:03,445
Then, off Port-au-Prince, Haiti, as a 250-bed

84
00:04:03,445 --> 00:04:07,539
hospital, supporting 35,000 Cuban and Haitian migrants.

85
00:04:07,989 --> 00:04:11,389
On September 11, 2001, it was activated that same

86
00:04:11,389 --> 00:04:12,082
afternoon.

87
00:04:12,082 --> 00:04:15,412
Three days later, it was at a pier in Manhattan,

88
00:04:15,412 --> 00:04:18,881
giving relief workers meals, a place to sleep, and

89
00:04:18,881 --> 00:04:22,212
medical and psychological care, until October 1.

90
00:04:22,662 --> 00:04:26,423
In 2003, during the Iraq War, Comfort spent 56 days

91
00:04:26,423 --> 00:04:29,963
in the Persian Gulf as a floating trauma center.

92
00:04:29,963 --> 00:04:33,430
It treated nearly 700 people, including wounded

93
00:04:33,430 --> 00:04:37,486
American troops, and about 200 injured Iraqi civilians.

94
00:04:37,936 --> 00:04:41,873
After Hurricane Katrina, in 2005, it treated nearly

95
00:04:41,873 --> 00:04:45,810
1,500 people in 12 days in Pascagoula, Mississippi,

96
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:48,049
then moved on to New Orleans.

97
00:04:48,499 --> 00:04:50,798
Comfort already knew Haiti.

98
00:04:50,798 --> 00:04:55,139
In April 2009, during a Continuing Promise mission,

99
00:04:55,139 --> 00:04:58,119
it spent 12 days in Port-au-Prince.

100
00:04:58,119 --> 00:05:02,461
Its staff treated 6,731 patients, and performed 161

101
00:05:02,461 --> 00:05:03,312
surgeries.

102
00:05:03,762 --> 00:05:06,902
In January 2010, an earthquake struck Haiti.

103
00:05:06,902 --> 00:05:10,328
Eight days later, Comfort arrived off the coast.

104
00:05:10,328 --> 00:05:13,967
By March, its teams had treated 1,025 patients, and

105
00:05:13,967 --> 00:05:15,680
performed 862 surgeries.

106
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:17,892
Nine babies were born on board.

107
00:05:18,342 --> 00:05:21,760
At the peak, on January 28, there were 411 patients

108
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:22,363
on board.

109
00:05:22,363 --> 00:05:25,847
At the height of the mission, more than 1,200 people

110
00:05:25,847 --> 00:05:27,322
were part of the crew.

111
00:05:27,772 --> 00:05:32,106
In 2017, after Hurricane Maria, it arrived in San

112
00:05:32,106 --> 00:05:34,937
Juan, Puerto Rico, on October 3.

113
00:05:34,937 --> 00:05:39,094
The Navy says it treated 1,899 patients on that

114
00:05:39,094 --> 00:05:42,367
mission, and performed 191 surgeries.

115
00:05:42,817 --> 00:05:45,942
The ship's master, Captain Roger Gwinn, described

116
00:05:45,942 --> 00:05:46,708
the arrival.

117
00:05:46,708 --> 00:05:49,833
When we first got there, there was no electricity

118
00:05:49,833 --> 00:05:51,364
and everything was dark.

119
00:05:51,814 --> 00:05:54,476
Two babies were born on board during that mission.

120
00:05:54,926 --> 00:05:56,307
Then came 2020.

121
00:05:57,107 --> 00:05:58,783
The mission was supposed to be simple.

122
00:05:59,233 --> 00:06:02,614
As COVID-19 spread, Comfort was sent to New York to

123
00:06:02,614 --> 00:06:05,861
serve as a referral hospital for patients who did

124
00:06:05,861 --> 00:06:06,855
not have COVID.

125
00:06:06,855 --> 00:06:10,567
That way, hospitals on shore could focus on COVID cases.

126
00:06:11,017 --> 00:06:12,662
It left Norfolk on March 28.

127
00:06:12,662 --> 00:06:15,013
Two days later, it tied up in Manhattan.

128
00:06:15,013 --> 00:06:18,246
For this mission, more than 1,200 people were on board.

129
00:06:18,696 --> 00:06:21,225
The first patient came aboard on April 1.

130
00:06:21,675 --> 00:06:23,256
Then the mission changed.

131
00:06:23,256 --> 00:06:26,417
On April 6, the ship began accepting patients with

132
00:06:26,417 --> 00:06:26,987
COVID-19.

133
00:06:26,987 --> 00:06:30,528
The Navy says it was reconfigured to treat all patients.

134
00:06:30,978 --> 00:06:33,934
It worked alongside the Javits Center and hospitals

135
00:06:33,934 --> 00:06:35,731
across New York and New Jersey.

136
00:06:36,181 --> 00:06:39,486
The mission commander, Captain Joseph O'Brien, said

137
00:06:39,486 --> 00:06:42,791
success hinged on coordination with FEMA, state and

138
00:06:42,791 --> 00:06:43,828
local officials.

139
00:06:44,278 --> 00:06:49,036
In one month, the Navy says, Comfort treated 182 patients.

140
00:06:49,036 --> 00:06:52,152
About 70 percent of them had COVID-19.

141
00:06:52,152 --> 00:06:56,254
Its teams performed more than 110 procedures, more

142
00:06:56,254 --> 00:07:00,519
than 540 x-rays and CT scans, and prepared more than

143
00:07:00,519 --> 00:07:03,718
1,300 intravenous and oral medications.

144
00:07:04,168 --> 00:07:07,247
For a thousand-bed ship, that's not a large number.

145
00:07:07,247 --> 00:07:10,266
But it shows something about how these ships work.

146
00:07:10,266 --> 00:07:13,346
The plan can change in the middle of a mission, and

147
00:07:13,346 --> 00:07:15,459
the hospital has to change with it.

148
00:07:15,909 --> 00:07:18,194
On April 30, Comfort left New York.

149
00:07:18,194 --> 00:07:21,588
Two days later, it was back in Norfolk, returning to

150
00:07:21,588 --> 00:07:24,787
what the Navy calls Ready 5 status, ready for the

151
00:07:24,787 --> 00:07:25,440
next call.

152
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:28,450
Not every mission starts with a disaster.

153
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:32,530
Since 2007, the Navy has run a humanitarian mission

154
00:07:32,530 --> 00:07:35,946
called Continuing Promise, in the Caribbean, and

155
00:07:35,946 --> 00:07:37,797
Central and South America.

156
00:07:37,797 --> 00:07:40,217
In 2025, it was the sixteenth one.

157
00:07:40,217 --> 00:07:41,427
Comfort's eighth.

158
00:07:41,877 --> 00:07:42,536
79 days.

159
00:07:42,536 --> 00:07:45,830
Six countries. Grenada. Panama. Ecuador.

160
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:48,713
The Dominican Republic. Costa Rica.

161
00:07:48,713 --> 00:07:50,689
And Trinidad and Tobago.

162
00:07:51,139 --> 00:07:54,554
The first mission stop was Grenada, the ship's

163
00:07:54,554 --> 00:07:55,964
second visit there.

164
00:07:55,964 --> 00:07:59,750
In seven days, the team treated 1,653 patients, and

165
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:02,348
performed 52 surgeries on the ship.

166
00:08:02,798 --> 00:08:04,727
Much of the work happened on shore.

167
00:08:04,727 --> 00:08:06,601
Teams set up medical sites ashore.

168
00:08:07,051 --> 00:08:10,800
There was medical care, eye care, and dentistry.

169
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:14,549
At these shore sites, the teams saw 1,919 dental

170
00:08:14,549 --> 00:08:18,376
patients, filled 17,166 prescriptions, and handed

171
00:08:18,376 --> 00:08:22,438
out more than 7,000 pairs of glasses and sunglasses.

172
00:08:22,888 --> 00:08:26,288
A dental assistant on the mission, Hospital Corpsman

173
00:08:26,288 --> 00:08:28,708
3rd Class Cody Romero, put it simply.

174
00:08:29,158 --> 00:08:32,304
Helping people out, and seeing them smile, and not

175
00:08:32,304 --> 00:08:34,003
in pain, is very rewarding.

176
00:08:34,453 --> 00:08:37,183
And some patients came out to the ship.

177
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:40,122
Surgeons performed 242 surgeries on board.

178
00:08:40,572 --> 00:08:44,469
Some of the work was done side by side with local

179
00:08:44,469 --> 00:08:45,583
medical teams.

180
00:08:45,583 --> 00:08:49,401
In five days in Panama, the team cared for 2,845

181
00:08:49,401 --> 00:08:52,901
people, and the Navy counted 43 side-by-side

182
00:08:52,901 --> 00:08:56,082
exchanges with Panamanian medical staff.

183
00:08:56,532 --> 00:08:58,208
Even the equipment got care.

184
00:08:58,208 --> 00:09:01,319
The ship's biomedical technicians restored more than

185
00:09:01,319 --> 00:09:04,071
2 million dollars' worth of medical equipment.

186
00:09:04,521 --> 00:09:08,791
Navy Seabees put in 2,571 work-hours improving eight

187
00:09:08,791 --> 00:09:09,941
local schools.

188
00:09:09,941 --> 00:09:13,390
The mission held 113 subject matter expert

189
00:09:13,390 --> 00:09:17,577
exchanges, and its medical exchanges drew more than

190
00:09:17,577 --> 00:09:20,862
4,000 participants across all its stops.

191
00:09:21,312 --> 00:09:23,826
Army veterinarians came too.

192
00:09:23,826 --> 00:09:28,406
They delivered 2,267 animal treatments, and trained

193
00:09:28,406 --> 00:09:30,202
512 animal handlers.

194
00:09:30,652 --> 00:09:34,853
In total, the Navy says, the mission treated 12,616

195
00:09:34,853 --> 00:09:35,595
patients.

196
00:09:36,045 --> 00:09:39,182
The mission commander, Captain Ryan Kendall, said

197
00:09:39,182 --> 00:09:41,615
the environment demanded adaptability.

198
00:09:41,615 --> 00:09:44,752
But our commitment to that promise never wavered.

199
00:09:45,202 --> 00:09:48,080
A hospital corpsman on board, Hospital Corpsman

200
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:51,142
Second Class Malachi Gregory, described the chance

201
00:09:51,142 --> 00:09:54,020
to come down here and help people who otherwise

202
00:09:54,020 --> 00:09:55,184
wouldn't be helped.

203
00:09:55,634 --> 00:10:00,137
On August 17, 2025, Comfort came home to Norfolk.

204
00:10:01,037 --> 00:10:03,534
So how do you keep a floating hospital ready?

205
00:10:03,984 --> 00:10:05,192
Not with one ship.

206
00:10:05,192 --> 00:10:06,132
With a system.

207
00:10:06,132 --> 00:10:07,408
An old tanker hull.

208
00:10:07,408 --> 00:10:11,302
A small standby crew that keeps it ready between missions.

209
00:10:11,302 --> 00:10:12,444
A five-day clock.

210
00:10:12,444 --> 00:10:15,600
And up to 1,200 Navy medical and communications

211
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:18,755
personnel, who come aboard when the call comes.

212
00:10:19,205 --> 00:10:23,531
Between them, the Navy says, Comfort and Mercy have

213
00:10:23,531 --> 00:10:27,263
treated more than 550,000 people since 2001.

214
00:10:27,713 --> 00:10:31,268
Each of those missions needed the same things.

215
00:10:31,268 --> 00:10:35,287
A ship ready at a pier, and people ready to fill it.

216
00:10:35,287 --> 00:10:35,751
A war.

217
00:10:35,751 --> 00:10:38,147
A terrorist attack. Hurricanes.

218
00:10:38,147 --> 00:10:40,156
An earthquake. A pandemic.

219
00:10:40,156 --> 00:10:44,098
And in between, a promise, kept one port at a time.

220
00:10:44,548 --> 00:10:46,048
Next time on KEPT ALIVE.

221
00:10:46,048 --> 00:10:48,299
The iron lung, and the end of polio.

222
00:10:49,099 --> 00:10:50,239
For education only.

223
00:10:50,239 --> 00:10:51,860
This is not medical advice.

224
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,681
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
